Sītā weeping answers grieving Rāma --- Sītā counts Rāma-named forest faults as virtues through love --- Sītā must follow by elders' command and would die by separation --- Sītā claims even Indra cannot assail her beside Rāma --- Sītā cites brāhmaṇas and beggar woman foretelling her forest dwelling --- Sītā claims the appointed hour makes the brāhmaṇa true --- Sītā deems forest conduct delightful and herself sinless through love --- Sītā claims husband as god and union beyond death --- Sītā vows poison fire or water if refused --- Rāma still refuses to lead her to the lonely forest
